<tr id="S1.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">CaricatureBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite></span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.3.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.3.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Ours</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.3.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.3.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;">✓</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.T1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Our model features user-friendly characteristics beyond dealing with the ID-shape conflict issue. Free-form conditioning: no additional requirements on shape conditions (if applicable) and ID conditions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deep Caricature Synthesis.</span> Caricature synthesis aims at generating a stylized portrait of a human subject with artistic styles and shape exaggerations while preserving the identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. CariGANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> utilizes GAN-based networks to generate stylized caricatures under the guidance of deformed facial landmarks. WarpGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> eases the warping process by using automatically generated control points. Several subsequent works include diversifying styles and exaggeration types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, using SENet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> to generate warping fields that densely guide the warping process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and introducing shape exaggeration blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> based on StyleGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> to achieve shape deformation by manipulating coarse-scale feature maps. Semantics CariGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> proposes using facial parsing maps to guide the shape transformation in the output. For works realizing the controllability of the shape exaggeration, DemoCaricature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> first proposes using free-hand sketches as a flexible and expressive medium to guide the shape deformation process. It encapsulates the identity information into textual embeddings and the key-value pathway of cross-attention blocks through per-sample tuning, but lacks consideration of its potential conflict with the shape information, limiting its performance on exaggerated free-hand sketches. CaricatureBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> represents a sketch with a fixed number of Bezier curves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and performs TPS deformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to generate synthetic data for training. However, it severely constrains the format of sketches and limits the diversity and creativity of shape conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to prior works, our approach explicitly addresses the fundamental ID-shape conflict through a novel training-free framework that balances identity preservation and shape exaggeration, overcoming the limitations observed in DemoCaricature. Furthermore, our method accepts free-hand sketches in their natural form, preserving the full expressive potential and creativity of sketch-based control while ensuring robustness to diverse and exaggerated shape conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T2I Personalization.</span> The goal of T2I personalization is to customize T2I models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> to consider concepts specified by a set of reference images while maintaining their original generalizability. Textual Inversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> finds new pseudo-word embeddings to represent the semantic features of referred subjects. DreamBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> fine-tunes pretrained Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> and Imagen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> models to link each specified subject with a unique representative textual identifier. For parameter-efficient fine-tuning, Custom Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> only fine-tunes cross-attention layers, while Perfusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> presents a Rank-One Model Editing (ROME) method that optimizes only the value-pathway in cross-attention layers. For multi-subject optimization, LatexBlend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> represents multiple concepts in a latent textual space, enabling multi-concept generation by retrieving concepts from a bank that can be freely combined without tuning. LoRACLR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> merges multiple pretrained LoRAs into a unified model using contrastive learning to align multiple concepts while preventing interference. For fast personalization without per-sample tuning, IP-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> trains lightweight and decoupled cross-attention blocks to embed image prompts into the model in parallel with textual prompts. InstantBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> achieves a tuning-free method by learning a network that can map reference images into aligned textual embeddings during inference. PuLID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> introduces a tuning-free approach that can output high-fidelity personalized images while mitigating the interruption to the behavior of the original model through contrastive alignment. Imagine yourself <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> proposes a parallel attention architecture with three text encoders and one vision encoder for high-fidelity identity preservation and text alignment. Flux already knows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> eliminates the need for pre-training and frames subject-driven generation as grid-based image completion with mosaic layouts by utilizing the generalizability of Flux models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Revisit Text-to-Image Diffusion Models</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> synthesize images from Gaussian noise by iterating the denoising process. They consist of two key components: a forward diffusion process and a reverse denoising process. The forward process corrupts a data sample <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by iteratively adding Gaussian noise <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> time-steps, producing noisy data <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The denoising process recovers <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by iteratively estimating and removing noise using a trained network <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes as input the predicted noisy latent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding time-step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
Typically, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> follows a UNET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> architecture and incorporates a series of residual blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, self-attention blocks, and cross-attention blocks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From the continuous perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> of diffusion models, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be explained as a scaled version of the score function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> that samples from the probability distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\hat{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\hat{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> through Langevin dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. When the generative process is combined with external conditions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding score function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C)</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into:
<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log\frac{q(\hat{z}_{t})q(C|\hat{z}_{t})}{q(C)}=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})+\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(C|\hat{z}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log\frac{q(\hat{z}_{t})q(C|\hat{z}_{t})}{q(C)}=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})+\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(C|\hat{z}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the first term <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an unconditional denoiser <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second term is the gradient guidance that can be generated by an energy function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mathcal{E}(\hat{z}_{t};t,C)=\log q(C|\hat{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mathcal{E}(\hat{z}_{t};t,C)=\log q(C|\hat{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By designing energy functions for different generation objectives, such as text-conditioned generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, sketch-guided generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, mask-guided generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, and image editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, the model can generate outputs that conform to specified conditions. Inspired by the success of prior works, we address the ID-shape conflict in caricature synthesis by designing tailored energy functions that explicitly guide noisy latent toward achieving an optimal trade-off.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>IP-Adapter and PuLID</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IP-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> introduces an effective approach to integrate ID features, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math>, into the model through the cross-attention mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> in parallel with textual features <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{txt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>txt</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{txt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There are two linear layers <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}^{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}^{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}^{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}^{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> into key (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and value (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math>) matrices. The
cross attention is then calculated as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}O=\mathrm{Attn}(Q,K_{\mathrm{id}},V_{\mathrm{id}})=\mathrm{Softmax}(\frac{Q{(K_{\mathrm{id}})^{T}}}{\sqrt{d}})V_{\mathrm{id}}\\
Q=W_{Q}h_{t},K_{\mathrm{id}}=W_{K}^{\mathrm{id}}C_{\mathrm{id}},V_{\mathrm{id}}=W_{V}^{\mathrm{id}}C_{\mathrm{id}},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Attn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>id</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msub><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>id</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}O=\mathrm{Attn}(Q,K_{\mathrm{id}},V_{\mathrm{id}})=\mathrm{Softmax}(\frac{Q{(K_{\mathrm{id}})^{T}}}{\sqrt{d}})V_{\mathrm{id}}\\
Q=W_{Q}h_{t},K_{\mathrm{id}}=W_{K}^{\mathrm{id}}C_{\mathrm{id}},V_{\mathrm{id}}=W_{V}^{\mathrm{id}}C_{\mathrm{id}},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}\in R^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}\in R^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents hidden feature maps at time-step <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> flattened along the height and width dimensions, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the latent dimension, and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=h\times w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=h\times w</annotation></semantics></math> represents the token number and is the product of the height <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> of the hidden feature maps. The output <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> is then added to the hidden feature maps. <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically extracted using pretrained image encoders, such as the CLIP Image encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> or face recognition models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. PuLID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> employs both encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> jointly and further emphasizes preserving the original model’s behavior by carefully controlling how <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> is injected using contrastive alignment losses, thereby preventing unintended contamination of the generation process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>T2I Adapter</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">T2I Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> presents a plug-and-play network that enables various spatial condition signals (e.g. sketches, edge maps, pose images, depth images) to guide the image generation process. The lightweight network consists of one convolution layer followed by two residual blocks at each scale. It maps input condition images into multi-scale condition features <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which capture spatial information at different levels of granularity. These extracted features are then added to the intermediate feature maps of the encoder within the UNET denoiser at each scale.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ID–shape conflict refers to the tendency of the diffusion model to favor either identity preservation or shape exaggeration when both conditions are provided, while failing to balance them. As illustrated in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 Methodology ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the ID-conditioned path <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally guides the denoising trajectory toward faithful identity preservation, whereas the shape-conditioned path <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> drives it toward precise caricature geometry. When both conditions <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are applied together, the ideal denoising trajectory should lie between these two extremes. However, since the regions near <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit higher probability densities than the intermediate region, the trajectory tends to collapse toward one side, overemphasizing either identity or shape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we proposed CaricHarmony by constructing three denoising trajectories: the two contrastive paths <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and the main path <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on both. For clarity, we denote intermediate features from <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> using superscripts <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while features from <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> are left without superscripts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We then impose inference-time cross-attention alignment guidance between the main path and each contrastive path, encouraging the main trajectory to stay within the region that balances identity and shape cues. The proposed gradient guidance mechanism explicitly steers the denoising direction away from biased extremes and toward the balanced region between <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively resolving the ID–shape conflict without any additional training. The overall architecture is shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4 Methodology ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13964v1/sampling_new.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/372;" width="685" height="372" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Dynamics of denoising paths in continuous sampling space of score-based diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Brighter color indicates areas where the denoised data is densely distributed. In blue (green) path, only <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is applied. Both conditions are applied in pink paths. The per-step gradient guidance from the energy function <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by red arrows.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13964v1/architecture_new.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/253;" width="651" height="253" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left: Inference pipeline of CaricHarmony</span>. The output path <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is jointly guided by two contrastive paths <math id="S4.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is only fed the binary sketch map <math id="S4.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as shape conditions and guides the shape alignment through <math id="S4.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which comprises <math id="S4.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>layout</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.F3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is only fed the ID conditions <math id="S4.F3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and guides the ID alignment through <math id="S4.F3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.F3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right: Cross-Attention Alignments.</span> Layout alignment obtains <math id="S4.F3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>layout</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}</annotation></semantics></math> by computing the token-wise differences between the query matrices from <math id="S4.F3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>. ID alignment obtains <math id="S4.F3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> by computing the token-wise differences between the ID cross attention outputs from <math id="S4.F3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The associations between the output tokens for alignment are built by computing the similarities of corresponding query tokens.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Shape Alignments</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Without identity conditions, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> fully encodes shape exaggeration according to the sketch. To ensure that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> retains this capability, we align its intermediate cross-attention features with those from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> during inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by PuLID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, we use the query features <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> from each cross-attention block as shape layout target, since they reflect how shape-related textual and sketch conditions are spatially grounded. By aligning <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model can learn to maintain the original layout distribution of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and render sufficiently exaggerated caricatures following the shape conditions. Specifically, we use a layout energy function <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>layout</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}</annotation></semantics></math> to perform alignment between <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=[q_{1},q_{2},...,q_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=[q_{1},q_{2},...,q_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\mathrm{s}}=[q_{1}^{\mathrm{s}},q_{2}^{\mathrm{s}},...,q_{n}^{\mathrm{s}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\mathrm{s}}=[q_{1}^{\mathrm{s}},q_{2}^{\mathrm{s}},...,q_{n}^{\mathrm{s}}]</annotation></semantics></math>:
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|q_{j}-q_{j}^{\mathrm{s}}\|_{2}^{2}\cdot c^{\mathrm{s}}_{j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">layout</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|q_{j}-q_{j}^{\mathrm{s}}\|_{2}^{2}\cdot c^{\mathrm{s}}_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\mathrm{s}}_{j}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\mathrm{s}}_{j}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a confidence weight indicating how strongly each token is associated with the sketch strokes. To compute weights, we first rasterize <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into a binary stroke map, where pixels on sketch strokes are assigned value <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and background pixels <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. We then resize this map to the spatial resolution of the query features to obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. This selectively preserves the shape cues tied to informative sketch strokes while suppressing irrelevant regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If the face shapes of two generated caricatures are closely matched, they should localize textual condition signals similarly in cross-attention blocks. The attention map <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q^{\mathrm{s}},Q^{\mathrm{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Attn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>txt</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q^{\mathrm{s}},Q^{\mathrm{s}})</annotation></semantics></math> of query <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to the textual key <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{txt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{txt}</annotation></semantics></math> is hence used as additional signals to guide <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> with semantic information:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}=\|\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q,Q)-\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q^{\mathrm{s}},Q^{\mathrm{s}})\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Attn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>txt</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Attn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>txt</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}=\|\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q,Q)-\mathrm{Attn}(K_{\mathrm{txt}},Q^{\mathrm{s}},Q^{\mathrm{s}})\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The combined shape energy is</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>layout</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>sem</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{layout}}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{sem}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>ID Alignment Guidance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Solely following the shape guidance from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> can lead to a significant degradation of ID fidelity. To improve ID consistency, previous works typically employ specialized loss functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, which compare ID features between generated and reference images using feature extractors trained solely on real-domain photographs. However, the exaggerated shapes of caricatures introduce a significant domain gap, thereby reducing the effectiveness of these methods. Additionally, the ID alignment guidance should be performed across intermediate time-steps, where the predicted images are often noisy and flawed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome the issue, we design a novel ID energy function tailored for caricature synthesis. We guide <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i+s}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the ID-conditioned cross-attention outputs from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O=[o_{1},...,o_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O=[o_{1},...,o_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\mathrm{i}}=[o_{1}^{\mathrm{i}},...,o_{n}^{\mathrm{i}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\mathrm{i}}=[o_{1}^{\mathrm{i}},...,o_{n}^{\mathrm{i}}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the outputs of cross-attention layers, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the target.
However, direct alignment is inefficient, since <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> may present a significantly different layout distribution compared to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> when conditioned on the exaggerated shape <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>O</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to guide <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> naively will lead to a layout mismatch and confuse the model.
Therefore, we build associations based on the relevance between query tokens.
For each <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find its corresponding <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> through
<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\arg\max_{l}\Phi(q_{j},q^{\mathrm{i}}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">l</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">l</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\arg\max_{l}\Phi(q_{j},q^{\mathrm{i}}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cosine similarity between two vectors.
The query-output pairs from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> behave like a ground-truth dictionary, where query tokens are “keys”, and output tokens are “values”. When a query token <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> arrives, we refer to the dictionary, look up <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> that best matches <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and retrieve its corresponding output token <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> to provide guidance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The ID energy is computed with a confidence weight <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> as
<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|o_{j}-o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}\|_{2}^{2}\cdot c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℰ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">id</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\|o_{j}-o_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}\|_{2}^{2}\cdot c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The confidence weight <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> aims to adapt the guidance strength:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}=\frac{\Phi(q_{j},q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}})-\phi}{1-\phi},\quad\phi=\min_{l}\Phi(q_{j},q^{\mathrm{i}}_{l})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>l</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\mathrm{i}}_{j}=\frac{\Phi(q_{j},q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}})-\phi}{1-\phi},\quad\phi=\min_{l}\Phi(q_{j},q^{\mathrm{i}}_{l}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">It helps weaken the guidance strength when the level of match between <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}^{\mathrm{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not distinguishable from the pool of target query tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13964v1/comparison.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/225;" width="685" height="225" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative performance comparison against caricature synthesis models.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Gradient Guidance with Balance Conditions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> are in opposition to each other. When the noisy latent is biased toward either side, the guidance strength from the other side increases, pulling the latent back to a balanced point. When combining both, the compositional energy function provides additional balance conditions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> and helps the model find a sweet point on harmonizing ID and shape conditions with explicit gradient guidance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let the existing conditions be <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathrm{e}}=[C_{\mathrm{txt}},C_{\mathrm{shape}},C_{\mathrm{id}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>txt</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>id</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathrm{e}}=[C_{\mathrm{txt}},C_{\mathrm{shape}},C_{\mathrm{id}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The score function in this work can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{b}},C_{\mathrm{e}})=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log\frac{q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{e}})q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|\hat{z}_{t},C_{\mathrm{e}})}{q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|C_{\mathrm{e}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{b}},C_{\mathrm{e}})=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log\frac{q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{e}})q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|\hat{z}_{t},C_{\mathrm{e}})}{q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|C_{\mathrm{e}})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{e}})+\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|\hat{z}_{t},C_{\mathrm{e}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(\hat{z}_{t}|C_{\mathrm{e}})+\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\log q(C_{\mathrm{b}}|\hat{z}_{t},C_{\mathrm{e}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We apply classifier-free guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to the first term and compute gradients of
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the second. The final guided noise prediction <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow(1+\gamma)\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t,C_{\mathrm{e}})-\gamma\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t,\emptyset)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow(1+\gamma)\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t,C_{\mathrm{e}})-\gamma\epsilon_{\theta}(\hat{z}_{t},t,\emptyset),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\epsilon}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\epsilon}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow\hat{\epsilon}_{t}+\eta\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}(\hat{z}_{t},t,C_{\mathrm{b}},C_{\mathrm{e}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow\hat{\epsilon}_{t}+\eta\nabla_{\hat{z}_{t}}\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{b}}(\hat{z}_{t},t,C_{\mathrm{b}},C_{\mathrm{e}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> controls classifier-free guidance and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> controls balance strength.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Timestep-Constrained Guidance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math> guides layout distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> at a coarse scale, while <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> guides detailed ID information at a relatively fine-grained scale. To fit the coarse-to-fine nature of the denoising process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, we only activate <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> falls in <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{start}},t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{end}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>start</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>end</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{start}},t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{end}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{start}},t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{end}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>start</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>end</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{start}},t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{end}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We encourage the guidance of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math> at early time intervals, setting <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{start}}=T=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>start</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{start}}=T=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{end}}=700" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>end</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>700</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{end}}=700</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> starts relatively late at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{start}}=900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>start</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{start}}=900</annotation></semantics></math> and ends at <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{end}}=400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>end</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\mathrm{i}}_{\mathrm{end}}=400</annotation></semantics></math> to prevent harming the quality of results fine-grained details <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
Our model is built on SDXL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> with the weights of Juggernaut-XL-v9 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. We use a pretrained ID Encoder from PuLID v1.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> and T2I-Sketch-Adapter-SDXL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to inject ID and shape conditions. During inference, the model is given textual prompts: “A highly exaggerated and detailed caricature of a man/woman.” The output image resolution is <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="768\times 768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>768</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>768</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">768\times 768</annotation></semantics></math>. We use DPM++ 2M sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> to accelerate inference speed, setting the number of inference steps <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to 40 and using a classifier-free guidance scale <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of 7. The guidance rate <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.4. The experiments are performed on a single RTX4090, and the code is implemented with PyTorch. All the other settings follow the original PuLID-ID-Encoder and T2I-Sketch-Adapter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset.</span>
We use the WebCaricature dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> to evaluate the performance of different models. Specifically, we consistently select the photo with the least sequence number for each identity as the reference ID image <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and extract the edge map from each caricature associated with the identity as the sketch image <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. The total number of samples is 1216.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
We use CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> scores to evaluate shape and ID consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: S-CLIP measures shape consistency by comparing edge maps of generated and ground-truth caricatures, while I-CLIP measures identity consistency between generated caricatures and identity images. To assess overall quality, we further compare our model with the other caricature synthesis models using ImageReward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and PickScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> as evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
The models for comparison include warping-based networks StyleCariGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, WarpGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and AutoToon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, also diffusion-based approaches DemoCaricature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and CaricatureBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Qualitative Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2 ID Alignment Guidance ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates the performance of our model against several caricature synthesis models. StyleCariGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, WarpGAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, and AutoToon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> cannot explicitly control the shape distortion. DemoCaricature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, similar to this work, introduces explicit shape control by leveraging free-hand sketches. CaricatureBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> guides the shape distortion with a fixed number of Bezier curves. Compared with the baselines, our model strikes a balance between preserving the facial features from identity images and ensuring a high level of creativity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5.2.1 User study ‣ 5.2 Quantitative Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> evaluates our method against several state-of-the-art diffusion-based models. DemoCaricature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> is the first diffusion-based model tailored for caricature synthesis. PuLID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is a tuning-free personalization model. We plug in a T2I-Sketch-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to provide additional shape control. Our model can generate high-quality caricatures in 16 seconds, which is approximately 4<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than DemoCaricature without per-sample tuning. Compared to PuLID, our results align substantially better with the provided shape conditions after balancing the shape and ID information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Quantitative Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth mentioning that CaricatureBooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> only accepts a fixed number of Bezier curves as input and cannot be applied to free-hand sketches and edge maps, which makes it incompatible with the experiment. The results are shown in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In 5.2 Quantitative Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>shape</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{shape}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{id}}</annotation></semantics></math> are disabled in turn, the model is biased toward ID preservation and shape exaggeration, and achieves the highest I-CLIP and S-CLIP score, respectively. By combining both, our final model finds a balanced point and generates results that respect both conditions (see <a href="#S6.F7" title="In 6 Ablation Study ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). As discussed before, custom evaluation models, including CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, cannot extract accurate facial features of caricatures because of the domain gap, causing I-CLIP to be highly sensitive to the level of shape distortion. Although the final model gets a slightly lower I-CLIP score compared to DemoCaricature, the results still show that our model can sufficiently preserve ID information under highly creative shape conditions (see <a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5.2.1 User study ‣ 5.2 Quantitative Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ CaricHarmony: Contrastive Diffusion Paths for Identity-Preserving Caricature Synthesis" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). Additionally, all our variants consistently outperform counterparts in ImageReward and PickScore due to SDXL’s strong generative capabilities, while their similar scores confirm that our gradient guidance preserves aesthetic quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.5pt;padding-right:5.5pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Methods</span></td>
